    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1247">

                <pb facs="1476" n="1278" sameAs="frus:frus1945Berlinv02/pg1278"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Nanking Embassy Files: Telegram</note>
                <head>No. 1247<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Hurley</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the President and the Secretary of State</hi><anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d1247-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">urgent</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Chungking</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1945-07-25">25 July 1945</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>(The following message is top secret and urgent for the eyes alone of the
                    Secretary of State, James F. Byrnes,
                    Potsdam, Germany, for the President from Ambassador Hurley.)</p>
                <p>Your message White House Number 311<anchor n="2" xml:id="d1247-f2"/> delivered to
                    Prime Minister Soong at his residence at
                    2130 hours,<anchor n="3" xml:id="d1247-f3"/> Chungking time, July 25th.
                    Generalissimo out of Chungking across Yangtze River in mountains. We intend to
                    complete translation and deliver message to him tonight.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1247-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d1247-f1-content">
                <p>The source copy does not indicate whether this message was sent via Army or Navy
                    channels.</p>
            </note>
